Open Source and the Spirit Bomb

Pantheon is shared so its useful patterns can travel.

Open source allows other people and machines to inspect the system, test it, fork it, challenge it, recombine it, and return improvements. The objective is not to prevent arrows from leaving. It is to create conditions in which arrows return sharper and the shared field becomes more capable.

The canonical repository still matters. Provenance, coherence, stewardship, trustworthy releases, community, evidence, and integration determine whether distributed pieces remain a pile of fragments or become a living system.

See the repository’s LICENSE, NOTICE, and related licensing artifacts for the controlling legal terms. Descriptions in documentation do not replace those terms.

Contribution Doctrine

A useful contribution should do one or more of the following:

implement a specified capability;

test an existing claim;

connect previously isolated components with evidence;

repair an inconsistency without erasing its history;

improve navigation or decompression;

add a real use case;

convert failure into reusable structure;

simplify the system without flattening it;

or preserve an important signal across time.

When adding a component, state:

What problem it addresses.

Which existing components it connects to.

Whether it is implemented, specified, experimental, or narrative.

How someone can test or falsify it.

What evidence would cause it to be revised or removed.

The Invitation

Pantheon is not completed by being read.

It becomes real through use: when a concept improves a decision, when a daemon reveals a missed relationship, when a failed route becomes a map, when intent survives a change in implementation, when one model carries a signal to another, or when an unfamiliar reader discovers a connection the original builders did not see.

The repository is the compressed object.

The reader is part of the decompression function.

Enter with a real question. Leave behind a better map.
